theory.intro:
The **gradient** measures how steep an interval is. It is the ratio $m = \dfrac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}}$: how far the interval goes up or down for each unit it moves to the right.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.3]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4,2.6);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (A) -- (B);
\draw[dashed,red] (A) -- (C) node[midway,below,black] {run};
\draw[dashed,red] (C) -- (B) node[midway,right,black] {rise};
\coordinate (p) at ($(C)!0.4cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (q) at ($(C)!0.4cm!(B)$);
\draw (p) -- ($(p)+(q)-(C)$) -- (q);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
An interval that slopes up from left to right has a positive rise, so its gradient is **positive**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\draw[->] (-0.7,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0.9,0.7) -- (3.5,2.9);
\fill (0.9,0.7) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (3.5,2.9) circle (2.5pt);
\node at (2.0,-1.1) {Positive gradient};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An interval that slopes down from left to right has a negative rise, so its gradient is **negative**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\draw[->] (-0.7,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0.9,2.9) -- (3.5,1.0);
\fill (0.9,2.9) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (3.5,1.0) circle (2.5pt);
\node at (2.0,-1.1) {Negative gradient};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

